.TH "RECORD\-TAIL\.SH" "" "June 2026" ""
.SH "NAME"
\fBrecord\-tail\.sh\fR
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBrecord\-tail\.sh\fR
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBrecord\-tail\.sh\fR streams the live stderr output of a running RECORD workflow using \fBtail \-f\fR\. It sources \fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR, resolves the stderr file descriptor of the running process via \fBget_stderr\fR, and follows it continuously until interrupted\.
.P
If \fBRECORD_START_PROGRAM\fR is running (the PID file exists and \fB/proc/<pid>\fR is present), the script calls \fBget_stderr\fR with the recorded PID and passes the resolved path to \fBtail \-f\fR, allowing live monitoring of the workflow's output\.
.P
If the program is not running \(em either because the PID file is absent or the recorded process no longer exists \(em a timestamped error is written to standard error and the script exits with status \-1\.
.SH "OPTIONS"
This script accepts no options or arguments\. All configuration is supplied via environment variables inherited from \fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR\.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Returns 0 when \fBtail \-f\fR is interrupted (e\.g\. by Ctrl\-C)\.
.P
Returns \-1 (typically 255) if:
.IP "\(bu" 4
the PID file does not exist; or
.IP "\(bu" 4
the process recorded in the PID file is no longer running\.
.IP "" 0
.P
Returns non\-zero if \fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR validation fails at source time (see \fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR documentation)\.
.SH "ENVIRONMENT"
.TP
\fBRECORD_START_PROGRAM\fR
The name of the workflow program being monitored\. Set and validated by \fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR at source time\.
.TP
\fBRECORD_DBTYPE\fR
Used by \fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR to construct the PID file path\.
.TP
\fBRECORD_PID_FILE\fR
Path to the PID file, exported by \fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR as \fB/tmp/record\.$USER\.$RECORD_DBTYPE\.$RECORD_START_PROGRAM\.pid\fR\.
.SH "EXAMPLES"
Follow the live output of a running workflow:
.IP "" 4
.nf
export RECORD_START_PROGRAM=my_sim
export RECORD_DBTYPE=sqlite3
record\-tail\.sh
.fi
.IP "" 0
.P
Use with \fBrecord\-errors\.sh\fR to check for errors after tailing:
.IP "" 4
.nf
record\-tail\.sh
record\-errors\.sh
.fi
.IP "" 0
.SH "FILES"
.TP
\fBlib\.record\.common\.sh\fR
Common library sourced at startup\. Must be present in the working directory or on \fBPATH\fR\.
.TP
\fB/tmp/record\.<USER>\.<RECORD_DBTYPE>\.<RECORD_START_PROGRAM>\.pid\fR
PID file identifying the running workflow process\. Read to obtain the PID passed to \fBget_stderr\fR\.
.SH "AUTHORS"
Doug Salt
.SH "REPORTING BUGS"
Report bugs to the maintainers of the RECORD workflow tools\.
.SH "BUGS"
\fBexit \-1\fR is used for error exits\. POSIX does not guarantee the behaviour of negative exit codes; most shells interpret \fB\-1\fR as 255, but this is not portable\.
.P
The \fB/proc/<pid>\fR liveness check is Linux\-specific and will not work on macOS or other non\-Linux systems\.
.P
\fBget_stderr\fR relies on \fB/proc/<pid>/fd/2\fR, which is also Linux\-specific\.
.P
The two error branches (PID file absent and process not running) produce identical messages and exit codes, making it impossible to distinguish between a workflow that was never started and one that has already finished\.
.P
The result of \fBget_stderr\fR is unquoted in the \fBtail \-f\fR call; if the resolved stderr path contains spaces, the command will fail or behave unexpectedly\.
.P
If the workflow process exits while \fBtail \-f\fR is running, \fBtail \-f\fR will continue to block rather than exit, as it is following a file rather than a pipe\. The user must interrupt it manually with Ctrl\-C\.
